{"success": true, "message": "Test details sent successfully", "data": {"test": {"_id": "69de21f17e39d99b57bc5b0e", "analysisVideoCdn": "yt", "exam": ["605dfcb2f226ac25a8bd5fef"], "ctype": null, "position": 0, "order": 12, "enableCalculator": false, "isContinuousQuestion": false, "reattemptAvailable": false, "singleSectionMultipleSubjects": false, "timeRestricted": false, "title": "Dual Nature of Matter - Test 5", "description": "Previous Year Questions as Chapter Test", "syllabus": "This test contains questions from all topics of this chapter included in the JEE Main syllabus. It includes a mix of questions from PYQs of JEE Main 2019 - 2025.", "totalTime": 50, "totalQuestions": 25, "totalMarks": 100, "bonusQuestions": null, "bonusMarks": null, "startDate": "2026-04-15T03:30:00.000Z", "endDate": "2027-04-30T00:00:00.000Z", "type": "free", "class": "605df635a37b7025c438d685", "instruction": "608289ffb160b022e9442c83", "subjects": [{"subjectId": "607018ee404ae53194e73d90", "totalMarks": 100, "bonusMarks": 0, "totalQuestions": 25, "bonusQuestions": 0, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5f4"}], "sections": [{"sectionId": {"_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5f1", "title": "Physics Single Correct", "sectionType": "normal", "subject": "607018ee404ae53194e73d90", "questions": [{"_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed571", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205a2"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b475f"], "concepts": ["60e2fad966f9ce3f243b4715"], "question": {"text": "The de-Broglie wavelength of an electron is the same as that of a photon. If velocity of electron is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>25</mn><mo>%</mo></math> of the velocity of light, then the ratio of K.E. of electron and K.E. of photon will be:"}, "options": [{"id": "978bd6", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>1</mn></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed572"}, {"id": "dedbd4", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>8</mn></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed573"}, {"id": "55875f", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>8</mn><mn>1</mn></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed574"}, {"id": "30dd0f", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed575"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed576", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205a2"], "topics": ["60d2d1d1185d7a32fa320586"], "concepts": ["60d2d1d0185d7a32fa320536"], "question": {"text": "A metallic surface is illuminated with radiation of wavelength <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#955;</mi></math>, the stopping potential is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math>. If the same surface is illuminated with radiation of wavelength <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mi>&#955;</mi></math>, the stopping potential becomes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mn>4</mn></mfrac></math>. The threshold wavelength for this metallic surface will be"}, "options": [{"id": "f03be4", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mi>&#955;</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed577"}, {"id": "b5f3a7", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mi>&#955;</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed578"}, {"id": "162eb6", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mi>&#955;</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed579"}, {"id": "fd3155", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi>&#955;</mi><mn>4</mn></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed57a"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed57b", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205a2"], "topics": ["60d2d1d1185d7a32fa320586"], "concepts": ["6117b64d478ae845c43c6843"], "question": {"text": "The stopping potential in the context of photoelectric effect depends on the following property of incident electromagnetic radiation:"}, "options": [{"id": "cba70c", "text": "Phase", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed57c"}, {"id": "976008", "text": "Intensity", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed57d"}, {"id": "c53459", "text": "Amplitude", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed57e"}, {"id": "21efd5", "text": "Frequency", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed57f"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed580", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205a2"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b475f"], "concepts": ["60e2fad966f9ce3f243b4715"], "question": {"text": "An electron (mass <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>m</mi></math>) with an initial velocity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mover><mi>v</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mover><mi>i</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mfenced><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#62;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mfenced></math> is moving in an electric field <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mover><mi>E</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mover><mi>i</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mfenced><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#62;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mfenced></math> where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math> is constant. If at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math>,&nbsp;de-Broglie wavelength is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac></math>, then its de-Broglie wavelength after time <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>t</mi></math> is given by"}, "options": [{"id": "760f7b", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed581"}, {"id": "20c737", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mfenced><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>e</mi><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mfenced></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed582"}, {"id": "533684", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>t</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed583"}, {"id": "a5e2c8", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mfenced><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>e</mi><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mfenced></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed584"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed585", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205a2"], "topics": ["60d2d1d1185d7a32fa320586"], "concepts": ["60e2fad966f9ce3f243b4706"], "question": {"text": "The magnetic field associated with a light wave is given, at the origin, by <math><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mi>&nbsp;</mi><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\" separators=\"|\"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin</mi></mrow><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mn>3.14</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>7</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mfenced><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin</mi></mrow><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mn>6.28</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>7</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mfenced><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfenced><mo>.</mo></math> If this light falls on a silver plate having a work function of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mo>.7</mo><mi>eV</mi><mo>,</mo></math> what will be the maximum kinetic energy of the photoelectrons?<br /><br><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>8</mn></msup><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>.6</mo><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>34</mn></mrow></msup><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mo>)</mo></math>"}, "options": [{"id": "b92543", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>6</mn><mo>.82</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#160;</mi><mi>eV</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed586"}, {"id": "73ef25", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>7</mn><mo>.72</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&nbsp;</mi><mi>eV</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed587"}, {"id": "2afea8", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>12</mn><mo>.5</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#160;</mi><mi>eV</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed588"}, {"id": "9b37f5", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>8</mn><mo>.52</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>eV</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed589"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed58a", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205a2"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b475f"], "concepts": ["60e2fad966f9ce3f243b4715"], "question": {"text": "An electron with mass ' $m$ ' with an initial velocity $(\\mathrm{t}=0) \\overrightarrow{\\mathrm{v}}=\\mathrm{v}_0 \\hat{\\mathrm{i}} \\quad\\left(\\mathrm{v}_0 \\gt 0\\right)$ enters a magnetic field $\\vec{B}=B_0 \\hat{j}$. If the initial de-Broglie wavelength at $\\mathrm{t}=0$ is $\\lambda_0$ then its value after time ' t ' would be :"}, "options": [{"id": "25c179", "text": "$\\frac{\\lambda_0}{\\sqrt{1-\\frac{\\mathrm{e}^2 \\mathrm{~B}_0^2 \\mathrm{t}^2}{\\mathrm{~m}^2}}}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed58b"}, {"id": "795e4e", "text": "$\\frac{\\lambda_0}{\\sqrt{1+\\frac{\\mathrm{e}^2 \\mathrm{~B}_0^2 \\mathrm{t}^2}{\\mathrm{~m}^2}}}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed58c"}, {"id": "1c04cc", "text": "$\\lambda_0 \\sqrt{1+\\frac{\\mathrm{e}^2 \\mathrm{~B}_0^2 \\mathrm{t}^2}{\\mathrm{~m}^2}}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed58d"}, {"id": "332381", "text": "$\\lambda_0$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed58e"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed58f", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205a2"], "topics": ["60d2d1d1185d7a32fa320586"], "concepts": ["60d2d1d0185d7a32fa320536"], "question": {"text": "<p>The electric field at a point associated with a light wave is given by<br /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>200</mn><mfenced open=\"[\" close=\"]\"><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mfenced><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>15</mn></msup></mrow></mfenced><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>sin</mi><mfenced><mrow><mn>9</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>15</mn></msup></mrow></mfenced><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfenced><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi>Vm</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math></p><p>Given:&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>14</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>eVs</mi></math></p><p>If this light falls on a metal surface having a work function of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>50</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>eV</mi></math>, the maximum kinetic energy of the photoelectrons will be</p>"}, "options": [{"id": "759dbc", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>90</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>eV</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed590"}, {"id": "0f8088", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>27</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>eV</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed591"}, {"id": "dc7264", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>60</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>eV</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed592"}, {"id": "9a8d76", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>42</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>eV</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed593"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed594", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205a2"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b475f"], "concepts": ["60e2fad966f9ce3f243b4715"], "question": {"text": "<p>The de Broglie wavelengths of a proton and an <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>α</mi></math> particle are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>λ</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mi>λ</mi></math> respectively. The ratio of the velocities of proton and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>α</mi></math> particle will be :</p>"}, "options": [{"id": "54b1bb", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo> </mo><mo>:</mo><mo> </mo><mn>8</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed595"}, {"id": "1cde97", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo> </mo><mo>:</mo><mo> </mo><mn>2</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed596"}, {"id": "63668c", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mo> </mo><mo>:</mo><mo> </mo><mn>1</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed597"}, {"id": "5ad4be", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>8</mn><mo> </mo><mo>:</mo><mo> </mo><mn>1</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed598"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed599", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205a2"], "topics": ["60d2d1d1185d7a32fa320586"], "concepts": ["6117b64d478ae845c43c6843"], "question": {"text": "<p>The variation of stopping potential <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mfenced></math> as a function of the frequency <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mi>&#957;</mi><mo>)</mo></math>&nbsp;of the incident light for a metal is shown in figure. The work function of the surface is</p><p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2F10f51844-a374-4e70-a58c-ad8e531cac7d.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 300px; height: 250px;\" /></p>"}, "options": [{"id": "0e07be", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>98</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>eV</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed59a"}, {"id": "0fe26e", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>07</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>eV</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed59b"}, {"id": "5faf81", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>36</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>eV</mi><mo>&#160;</mo></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed59c"}, {"id": "08b3a3", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>18</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>eV</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed59d"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed59e", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205a2"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b475f"], "concepts": ["60e2fad966f9ce3f243b4715"], "question": {"text": "An <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#945;</mi></math> particle and a proton are accelerated from rest by a potential difference of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>200</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math>. After this, their de Broglie wavelengths are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>&#945;</mi></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></math> respectively. The ratio <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>&#945;</mi></msub></mfrac></math> is :"}, "options": [{"id": "6f5370", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>8</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed59f"}, {"id": "7e39cf", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>8</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5a0"}, {"id": "a3face", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>8</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5a1"}, {"id": "22be9c", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>7</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>8</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5a2"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5a3", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205a2"], "topics": ["60d2d1d1185d7a32fa320586"], "concepts": ["60e2fad966f9ce3f243b4706"], "question": {"text": "In a photoelectric effect experiment, the threshold wavelength of light is<math><mi>&#160;</mi><mn>380</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#160;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math> . If the wavelength of incident light is<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>260</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#160;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math> , the maximum kinetic energy of emitted electrons will be<br />Given <math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E</mi></math> (in <math><mi>&#160;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math> ) <math><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1237</mn></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#955;</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#160;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mfrac></math>"}, "options": [{"id": "2987ca", "text": "<math><mn>4.5</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"> </mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5a4"}, {"id": "38ea7c", "text": "<math><mn>3.0</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"> </mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5a5"}, {"id": "0fcde1", "text": "<math><mn>1.5</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"> </mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5a6"}, {"id": "4da82e", "text": "<math><mn>15.1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"> </mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5a7"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5a8", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205a2"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b475f"], "concepts": ["60e2fad966f9ce3f243b4715"], "question": {"text": "A proton of mass ' $m_{\\mathrm{p}}$ ' has same energy as that of a photon of wavelength ' $\\lambda$ '. If the proton is moving at non-relativistic speed, then ratio of its de Broglie wavelength to the wavelength of photon is."}, "options": [{"id": "aaa52e", "text": "$\\frac{1}{\\mathrm{c}} \\sqrt{\\frac{\\mathrm{E}}{\\mathrm{m}_{\\mathrm{p}}}}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5a9"}, {"id": "df960d", "text": "$\\frac{1}{\\mathrm{c}} \\sqrt{\\frac{2 \\mathrm{E}}{\\mathrm{m}_{\\mathrm{p}}}}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5aa"}, {"id": "9866cb", "text": "$\\frac{1}{2 c} \\sqrt{\\frac{E}{m_p}}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5ab"}, {"id": "3708ca", "text": "$\\frac{1}{\\mathrm{c}} \\sqrt{\\frac{\\mathrm{E}}{2 \\mathrm{~m}_{\\mathrm{p}}}}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5ac"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5ad", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205a2"], "topics": ["60d2d1d1185d7a32fa320586"], "concepts": ["60d2d1d0185d7a32fa320536"], "question": {"text": "The work function of a metal is 3 eV. The color of the visible light that is required to cause emission of photoelectrons is"}, "options": [{"id": "810187", "text": "Green", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5ae"}, {"id": "3dcc8f", "text": "Blue", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5af"}, {"id": "c0826c", "text": "Red", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5b0"}, {"id": "2d716c", "text": "Yellow", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5b1"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5b2", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205a2"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b475f"], "concepts": ["60e2fad966f9ce3f243b4715"], "question": {"text": "A proton and an electron are associated with same de-Broglie wavelength. The ratio of their kinetic energies is:\n(Assume h=6.63 $\\times 10^{-34} \\mathrm{~J} \\mathrm{~s}, \\mathrm{~m}_{\\mathrm{e}}=9.0 \\times 10^{-31} \\mathrm{~kg}$ and $\\mathrm{m}_{\\mathrm{p}}=1836$ times $\\mathrm{m}_{\\mathrm{e}}$ )"}, "options": [{"id": "edd64a", "text": "$1: \\sqrt{1836}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5b3"}, {"id": "6bd104", "text": "$1: \\frac{1}{1836}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5b4"}, {"id": "b6d223", "text": "$1: \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1836}}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5b5"}, {"id": "05d5d1", "text": "1: 1836", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5b6"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5b7", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205a2"], "topics": ["60d2d1d1185d7a32fa320586"], "concepts": ["6117b64d478ae845c43c6843"], "question": {"text": "The threshold frequency of metal is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math>. When the light of frequency <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math> is incident on the metal plate, the maximum velocity of photoelectron is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math>. When the frequency of incident radiation is increased to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math>. the maximum velocity of photoelectrons emitted is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>. The ratio of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;is:"}, "options": [{"id": "911986", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5b8"}, {"id": "6efdd2", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>8</mn></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5b9"}, {"id": "f3a481", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>16</mn></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5ba"}, {"id": "fba8d4", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5bb"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5bc", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205a2"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b475f"], "concepts": ["60e2fad966f9ce3f243b4715"], "question": {"text": "An electron of mass <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></math> and a proton of mass <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1836</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></math> are moving with the same speed. The ratio of their de Broglie wavelength <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>o</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>o</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>&#160;</mo></mrow></msub></mfrac></math> will be :"}, "options": [{"id": "611db3", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1836</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5bd"}, {"id": "8a36e2", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>1836</mn></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5be"}, {"id": "1873f5", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>918</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5bf"}, {"id": "9c020f", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5c0"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5c1", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205a2"], "topics": ["60d2d1d1185d7a32fa320586"], "concepts": ["60e2fad966f9ce3f243b4706"], "question": {"text": "Two streams of photons, possessing energies to five and ten times the work function of metal are incident on the metal surface successively. The ratio of the maximum velocities of the photoelectron emitted, in the two cases respectively, will be"}, "options": [{"id": "8af796", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>:</mo><mn>2</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5c2"}, {"id": "202dd1", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>:</mo><mn>3</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5c3"}, {"id": "438701", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>:</mo><mn>3</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5c4"}, {"id": "d86e8c", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mo>:</mo><mn>2</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5c5"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5c6", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205a2"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b475f"], "concepts": ["60e2fad966f9ce3f243b4715"], "question": {"text": "A proton moving with one tenth of velocity of light has a certain de Broglie wavelength of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#955;</mi></math>. An alpha particle having certain kinetic energy has the same de-Brogle wavelength <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#955;</mi></math>. The ratio of kinetic energy of proton and that of alpha particle is :"}, "options": [{"id": "a38a15", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>:</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>1</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5c7"}, {"id": "47ed59", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>:</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>1</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5c8"}, {"id": "f1f25b", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>:</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>2</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5c9"}, {"id": "8dc66e", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>:</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>4</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5ca"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5cb", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205a2"], "topics": ["60d2d1d1185d7a32fa320586"], "concepts": ["60e2fad966f9ce3f243b4706"], "question": {"text": "The surface of certain metal is first illuminated with light of wavelength <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>350</mn><mi>&nbsp;</mi><mn>n</mn><mn>m</mn></math> and then, by a light of wavelength <math><msub><mrow><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>540</mn><mi>&nbsp;</mi><mn>n</mn><mn>m</mn><mo>.</mo></math> It is found that the maximum speed of the photoelectrons in the two cases differ by a factor of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn></math>.&nbsp;The work function of the metal (in <math><mn>e</mn><mn>V</mn></math>) is close to<br /><br>(Energy of photon <math><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1240</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&nbsp;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></mrow></mfenced></mrow></mfrac><mi>&nbsp;</mi><mn>e</mn><mn>V</mn></math> )"}, "options": [{"id": "a6d17e", "text": "<math><mn>2.5</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5cc"}, {"id": "0d0dbb", "text": "<math><mn>1.8</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5cd"}, {"id": "8e3080", "text": "<math><mn>5.6</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5ce"}, {"id": "633baa", "text": "<math><mn>1.4</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5cf"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5d0", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205a2"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b475f"], "concepts": ["60e2fad966f9ce3f243b4715"], "question": {"text": "An electron of mass <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"italic\">e</mi></msub></math> and a proton of mass <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"italic\">P</mi></msub></math> are accelerated through the same potential difference. The ratio of the de-Broglie wavelength associated with the electron to that with the proton is&nbsp;"}, "options": [{"id": "59dce3", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5d1"}, {"id": "a9ca1f", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5d2"}, {"id": "b4bc99", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msqrt><mfrac><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mfrac></msqrt></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5d3"}, {"id": "d69f15", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><msub><mi mathvariant=\"italic\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"italic\">e</mi></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"italic\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"italic\">p</mi></msub></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5d4"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5d5", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205a2"], "topics": ["60d2d1d1185d7a32fa320586"], "concepts": ["60d2d1d0185d7a32fa320536"], "question": {"text": "<p>With reference to the observations in photo-electric effect, identify the correct statements from below:</p><p>A. The square of maximum velocity of photoelectrons varies linearly with frequency of incident light.</p><p>B. The value of saturation current increases on moving the source of light away from the metal surface.</p><p>C. The maximum kinetic energy of photo-electrons decreases on decreasing the power of LED (light emitting diode) source of light.</p><p>D. The immediate emission of photo-electrons out of metal surface can not be explained by particle nature of light/electromagnetic waves.<br />E. Existence of threshold wavelength can not be explained by wave nature of light/electromagnetic waves.</p><p>Choose the correct answer from the options given below:</p>"}, "options": [{"id": "8b4645", "text": "A and B only", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5d6"}, {"id": "879f38", "text": "A and E only", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5d7"}, {"id": "39ddf5", "text": "C and E only", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5d8"}, {"id": "f70f54", "text": "D and E only", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5d9"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5da", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205a2"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b475f"], "concepts": ["60e2fad966f9ce3f243b4715"], "question": {"text": "A proton and an electron have the same de Broglie wavelength. If $\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{p}}$ and $\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{e}}$ be the kinetic energies of proton and electron respectively, then choose the correct relation :"}, "options": [{"id": "351f96", "text": "$K_p>K_e$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5db"}, {"id": "603d55", "text": "$\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{p}} < \\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{e}}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5dc"}, {"id": "f1aa8a", "text": "$\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{p}}=\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{e}}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5dd"}, {"id": "b22a71", "text": "$\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{p}}=\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{e}}{ }^2$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5de"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5df", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205a2"], "topics": ["60d2d1d1185d7a32fa320586"], "concepts": ["6117b64d478ae845c43c6843"], "question": {"text": "Given below are two statements :\n<img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2FVv8V7jULCpikDwS2f-JwFuUXGOGm5PoxVNNvDZEQf_s.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\"/>\nStatement I : Figure shows the variation of stopping potential with frequency $(v)$ for the two photosensitive materials $\\mathrm{M}_1$ and $\\mathrm{M}_2$. The slope gives value of $\\frac{\\mathrm{h}}{\\mathrm{e}}$, where $\\mathrm{h}$ is Planck's constant, $\\mathrm{e}$ is the charge of electron.\nStatement II : $\\mathrm{M}_2$ will emit photoelectrons of greater kinetic energy for the incident radiation having same frequency.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below."}, "options": [{"id": "e67f8d", "text": "Both Statement I and Statement II are correct", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5e0"}, {"id": "d75f84", "text": "Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5e1"}, {"id": "d992c1", "text": "Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5e2"}, {"id": "741f02", "text": "Statement I is correct and Statement II is incorrect", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5e3"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5e4", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205a2"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b475f"], "concepts": ["60e2fad966f9ce3f243b4715"], "question": {"text": "<p>The kinetic energy of an electron,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#945;</mi><mo>-</mo></math>particle and a proton are given as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>K</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>K</mi></math> respectively. The de-Broglie wavelength associated with electron <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>)</mo><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>&#945;</mi><mo>-</mo></math>particle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>&#945;</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></math>&nbsp;and the proton <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></math> are as follows:</p>"}, "options": [{"id": "678062", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>&#945;</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>&#62;</mo><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5e5"}, {"id": "99649f", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>&#945;</mi></msub><mo>&#60;</mo><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>&#60;</mo><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5e6"}, {"id": "fd64b8", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>&#945;</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>&#60;</mo><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5e7"}, {"id": "41d85c", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>&#945;</mi></msub><mo>&#62;</mo><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>&#62;</mo><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5e8"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5e9", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205a2"], "topics": ["60d2d1d1185d7a32fa320586"], "concepts": ["60d2d1d0185d7a32fa320536"], "question": {"text": "In photoelectric effect, the stopping potential $\\left(\\mathrm{V}_0\\right) \\mathrm{v} / \\mathrm{s}$ frequency $(\\nu)$ curve is plotted.\n( h is the Planck's constant and $\\phi_0$ is work function of metal )\n(A) $\\mathrm{V}_0 \\mathrm{v} / \\mathrm{s} \\nu$ is linear.\n(B) The slope of $\\mathrm{V}_0 \\mathrm{v} / \\mathrm{s} \\nu$ curve $=\\frac{\\phi_0}{\\mathrm{~h}}$\n(C) h constant is related to the slope of $\\mathrm{V}_0 \\mathrm{v} / \\mathrm{s} \\nu$ line.\n(D) The value of electric charge of electron is not required to determine $h$ using the $V_0 \\mathrm{v} / \\mathrm{s} \\nu$ curve.\n(E) The work function can be estimated without knowing the value of $h$.\n\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :"}, "options": [{"id": "aad1cc", "text": "(C) and (D) only", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5ea"}, {"id": "036516", "text": "(A), (C) and (E) only", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5eb"}, {"id": "6f11e1", "text": "(A), (B) and (C) only", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5ec"}, {"id": "3d1343", "text": "(D) and (E) only", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5ed"}], "questionLabels": []}], "markingScheme": {"_id": "608283e223cf4b3ad8f125e6", "title": "JEE Single Correct", "correctAttempt": 4, "incorrectAttempt": -1, "createdAt": "2021-04-09T08:45:27.797Z", "updatedAt": "2021-07-05T05:39:15.785Z", "__v": 0, "updated_by": "6042252509c5bf34e8a2f178"}, "maximumAttemptLimit": 25, "linkedQuestions": [], "createdAt": "2026-04-14T14:28:00.471Z", "updatedAt": "2026-04-14T14:28:00.471Z", "__v": 0}, "position": 0, "_id": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5f3"}], "bonusSections": [], "packDetails": [], "paidFeatures": [], "tags": [], "timeRestrictionSlots": [], "createdAt": "2026-04-14T11:16:01.428Z", "updatedAt": "2026-04-17T04:58:01.312Z", "testType": "PYQs as Chapter Tests", "isResumeBufferAllowed": true, "enableAccessibility": true, "maxAttemptableQuestion": 25, "analysisVideoThumbnail": "", "analysisVideoThumbnailDark": "", "shortTitle": "", "subjectDetails": [{"subjectId": "607018ee404ae53194e73d90", "title": "Physics", "shortName": "Phy"}], "testTimerType": "mm", "isMarkForReviewAvailable": true}, "temporaryTestDetail": {"_id": "6a4a1e7a9cbf3c65130461de", "testId": "69de21f17e39d99b57bc5b0e", "userId": "6914cc9c4d7566258ee3a8fd", "startedAt": "2026-07-05T09:06:02.239Z", "lastAttemptedSection": null, "totalTimeLeft": 50, "bonusAttempt": false, "sections": [{"sectionId": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed5f1", "questions": [{"questionId": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed571", "markedOptions": [], "inputValue": null, "timeTaken": 0, "timeLeftWhenAttempted": null, "status": "notVisited", "_id": "6a4a1e7a9cbf3c65130461e0", "uid": "6a4a1e7a9cbf3c65130461e1"}, {"questionId": "69de4ef0c677dc36a88ed576", "markedOptions": [], "inputValue": null, "timeTaken": 0, "timeLeftWhenAttempted": null, "status": "notVisited", "_id": "6a4a